{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: R7 certified rules policy, head d73ca9c5bcbcd8a971ef14affc59bbcefad97a9b, tree b4dbac71198f26f84513966c58d9cef7f248a0b2, branch builder/security-r7-certified-policy-clean-20261026: 61 commits on Main 9daa037e, 85 paths, all 85 named by the artifacts. Review request cf8bf8fc, checker promise eafb7667, request 0716e75a (builder promise 7e959e25). Closes findings 3.2, 3.3 and 3.4 of 6dd06d03; finding 2.2 belongs to the earlier R6 head.\n\nRead order: the primary note at the head in full; request 0716e75a, planner guidance 34c8885a, the builder's question 53707834 and the planner ruling 036e7f12; the production diff (rules-policy-v11, rules-policy-tree, rules-certificate, rules-scope-v11, destination-v14, reservation-v14, the Destination 14 branches of destination.ts, destination-host, rules-tree-provider, the two hosts, archive values and archive policy, the kernel change in derive/outcomes.ts, the git names split, the catalog, versions and Page); the required witness tests; then the evidence. An independent security read of the required-check path, the certificate operation slots and the policy and tree binding ran in parallel; its result is folded into the Security section.\n\nValidation (docs/testing.md, \"Follow-up after a gate\"; planner ruling 036e7f12). I assessed the four conditions at the exact head and all four hold:\n1. Each repair changed only the failing test file. Between gate V3 at 8ba23a34 and the last execution checkpoint 5a816922, the only source paths that change are packages/platform/test/rules-scope.test.ts (one line and its comment: platform:rules@11 to @12 as the next unsupported version) and packages/scope/test/archive-target.test.ts (one line: platform:register@19 to @20). The note and the follow-up manifest are the only other changes. No production code, configuration, dependency, shared helper or imported code changed after V3. Neither test file is imported. Two files name them without importing: definition-corpus/fixture-origin-aliases.ts cites rules-scope.test.ts lines 185, 337 and 341, which the repair does not move; scripts/workspace-rename-preservation.json records historical digests that only scripts/rename-workspaces.mjs reads, and no test reads that file or that script.\n2. After each repair the owning compiler context, the whole owning file and the unreached steps ran. Rules repair at ae49c5a6: platform tsconfig.test.json exit 0, the whole rules-scope file 9 of 9, then the native stage that V3 never reached. Archive repair at 5a816922: scope tsconfig.test.json exit 0, the whole archive-target file 7 of 7. Native is the last stage of scripts/test.mjs and the test step is the last step of gate.sh; only the EXIT-trap summary reporter follows it, so no gate step remains unrun. The follow-up used the same command as the gate's native stage (vitest run --config vitest.native.config.ts).\n3. The note keeps the outcomes separate: V3 failed (plain 2,834 passed, 1 failed, 1 skipped; native unreached), the native follow-up failed (778 passed, 2 failed, 2 skipped, both failures archive-target.test.ts:52), and the two repaired whole files passed. It does not call V3 a pass.\n4. Every case, deadline and production assertion is kept; each sentinel only moves the version expected to be unsupported, because this head makes Rules 11 and Register 19 supported.\nEvery gate step other than plain and native passed in V3 at 8ba23a34 (whitespace, commit messages, helpers, citations, doc links, notes with 0 warnings, typecheck, kernel, Node 57, Counting 55), and production is identical from 8ba23a34 to the head. The head differs from 5a816922 only in the note and the follow-up manifest (tree 4824d97f to b4dbac71), so the documents-only rule applies and no second gate is owed. The four commits after V3 never passed the gate's message and whitespace checks; I checked them: subjects are 48 to 59 characters (the longest on the branch is 83) and git diff --check is clean over the branch.\n\nArchitecture: sound.\n- The new family is explicit and additive: Register 19, Directory 18, Membership 8, Rules 11, Destination 14 and Inbox 1, behind --cohort archive-certified-rules. Default, public and Grow pins do not change, and no hosted PLATFORM_CREATION_PINS list names the new versions, so hosted Workers cannot create it until the planner allows it. Every historical branch keeps its code: Destination 14 work is gated on the exact pin, and older versions take the unchanged paths.\n- Policy (3.2, 3.3). One strict decoder produces the owned complete policy and one digest in artroom-rules-policy-1, keeping explicit false apart from absence. ownerMayReview is a required boolean on establish and publish, and Directory 18 creates Rules 11 with an explicit false. Zero approvals need an explicit opt-in at the top level and per extent; the protected rules extent can never be zero. Every extent approver must be change.review, and the old judgment still adds rules.publish for the rules extent. An extent pattern must match a leaf of the certified tree unless that occurrence is listed in futurePatterns, which must be a subset of the extent's own patterns.\n- Tree proof. The proof binds rules, proposal, destination, repository, ref and commit, takes the root from the commit bytes, requires every supplied tree to be reached and no tree to be missing, and bounds bytes, visits, depth, path bytes and matching work. The provider side mirrors the bounds and the native side verifies again. Certification happens only while the destination head stays at the opening entry, checked before and after the provider read, and publish re-verifies the proof, the certificate outcome and the sibling family.\n- Required checks with extents (3.4): the head chose enforcement at the destination. At list time Destination 14 captures the complete policy, the touched extents and the full required descriptors (global required checks plus the touched-extent union) into the publication's passes record. Check results count only with a checker proof bound to that capture (configuration, checker MemberRef, tree, proposal and a fresh active key observation). Stage, resend and push all wait for complete14. A lane's rules copy no longer matters for admission, which is what the stale-lane witness shows; the lane protocol keeps its historical five-field extent summary, which never carried patterns.\n- The kernel change in derive/outcomes.ts is a real repair: synthetic effect and send rules used to replace the pinned native rules, hiding native type callbacks. They now sit beside them under fresh names that cannot collide.\n\nSecurity: no defect found in the request's scope; three limits to record.\n- Required checks. The judge reads Destination 14's own fresh Rules observation (at most 10 s old, envelope digest equal to the observed extents, policy re-decoded to its proposal, every projected field equal), never the lane's copy. Unconfigured policy refuses rules-not-configured. The required set is every global required check plus the touched-extent union; an unmatched path falls to the one mandatory catch-all extent, and refused or unreadable paths leave rules unmet. Every later use revalidates the captured record against manifest, tree, destination and requiredChecks; a result counts only for the current fenced job with the captured configuration, checker and tree and a current checker key, so jobs from an earlier staged tree do not count. No G14 passes value is written without its captured policy. The non-staged path runs judgeReservationV14, where earlier refusals win and success needs every required check met.\n- Certificate operation slots. The act adds exactly one certify-rules-tree operation to its branch and opens exactly one, with one attempt; the outcome writes no effects and opens nothing; the host answers attempt 1 only. No count is reset, refunded or raised, and the publication's slot counts do not change (only the type of passes does). Repeated requests each yield at most one certificate and need rules.publish, as read-token does; replaying an answer cannot produce a second one.\n- Binding. All six binding fields come from native branch and family birth facts, not from the proof; the commit bytes must hash to the bound commit; publish re-checks the signed fields, the retained policy and the proposal, the certificate outcome immediately after its request, and the proof.\n- No new authority: no secret, setting or route is added; the provider token is read-scoped and revoked after the tree read; the new archive places are public configuration values with stated maxima. The seals reproduce (security-entry-points 4 of 4).\nLimits, nonblocking, for the planner to place:\n1. File publication. Destination 14 inherits publish-file from Destination 4 (usesFilePublicationProtocol includes @14; fileJudge reserves and pushes with no policy or check evaluation). An installed application may still write any path, including the rules extent, with no review and no required check. This is the documented Destination 4 model (packages/platform/README.md: \"Change review rules do not govern these writes\"), not a regression, and finding 3.4 names only the reservation path. But the note says, without qualification, that Destination 14 \"enforces both global required checks and checks required by touched extents\". The note should say this holds for change publications only, and the planner should decide whether a certified destination should admit publish-file at all.\n2. Certificate reuse. A certificate binds one commit and is never marked used, so the same proposal can be published again later with it, after the repository has changed. Patterns are proven to match only at the certified commit. Re-publishing still needs rules.publish. This matches the note (\"verifies a captured head, not the freshness of a later publish\").\n3. Certification can be stalled. Any entry in the destination during the tree read (a read-token act, which every role can make) leaves the certificate unanswered, and the member must file again. This is liveness only; a member could repeat it on purpose.\n\nSimplification: acceptable, with notes.\n- The head is large (+4,961/-242, about 1,600 production lines) and dense; the long single-line conditions in rules-certificate.ts and destination.ts are hard to audit. That is the style of the surrounding native code, so not blocking.\n- The head carries two manifests. The 25 October one is the historical index of 132 earlier outputs and the 26 October one indexes the delivery directory. Both verify, but one manifest per head would be simpler to check.\n- destination.ts now carries three generations of publication-state handling (array, D1 standing, G14 certified). Retiring older shapes is TD work, not this head's.\n\nEvidence, verified by me:\n- Follow-up manifest digest 4d4eb0bf… matches the review request. All 60 files in it match their size and SHA-256 digest; nothing in /Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-10-26-certified-rules-delivery is writable (0 of 70 entries) and all 59 SHA256SUMS lines pass.\n- Earlier manifest (digest 2b21a6b6…): all 132 files match; nothing in /Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-10-25-certified-rules-policy is writable (0 of 1,121 entries) and all 953 SHA256SUMS lines pass.\n- All 22 SHA-256 digests cited in the note appear in the two manifests. The note is 26,789 bytes.\n- From the logs: V3 result.json exitCode 1 for expectedHead 8ba23a34 and expectedTree 1fe50e37, stderr empty, the one failure at rules-scope.test.ts:79 (platform(\"platform:rules@11\") now returns a definition). The native log ends \"Tests 2 failed | 778 passed | 2 skipped (782)\", both failures at archive-target.test.ts:52 expecting unsupported-foreign-copy for Register 19. The repaired files end \"9 passed (9)\" and \"7 passed (7)\".\n- scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs passes 4 of 4 at the head in the builder's worktree (1.24 s), and the worktree stayed clean.\n- V17, the paired controls, the declaration generations and the Page build are cited by digest and were not rerun; I read their records only. Timings and load figures are the builder's observations.\n\nRollout notes for the planner (nonblocking):\n1. Repository size. The certificate must hold the commit and every tree within 196,608 decoded bytes and 262,144 canonical bytes. This repository at Main has 181 trees and about 166 KB of tree bytes, which fits; a moderately larger repository cannot configure Rules 11. Check the demo repository before scheduling the cohort.\n2. Quiet window. A certificate is answered only if no other entry lands in the destination while the tree is read. If one does, the host gives no answer, and the member must file a new certificate request, which carries its own operation slot. Busy destinations may need retries (see Security limit 3).\n2a. Record size. certifiedPublicationState admits a passes record up to 600,000 bytes, but every entry is limited to 256 KiB (Bounds.entryBytes, enforced in turn.ts as Unfit \"size\"), and each fence and check result rewrites the whole record. By my estimate the largest policy the decoder admits is about 150 KB, so the record should fit, with a thin margin. If it does not, the entry is refused, which fails closed but holds that publication. Tie the ceiling to the entry bound, or add a witness with a maximum-size policy, in a later head.\n3. Page says \"Use the certified rules workflow to change them\", but no user-facing publish path for Rules 11 ships in this head. Name the actual command when one exists, or reword.\n4. Planner ruling 036e7f12 asks the note to record, as a limit owned by BR-7, that a test naming \"the next unsupported version\" goes stale whenever a version is added. The note at this head does not say so yet. One documents-only follow-up commit can add it together with the file-publication qualification (Security limit 1), without a second gate; the planner can also hold both in their owning requests.\n5. Deploy: no new setting and no redeploy step; hosted creation stays closed to the new family until the planner changes PLATFORM_CREATION_PINS with the grow demo cohort. Existing live scopes keep their pins.\n\nVerdict: approved at exact head d73ca9c5 / tree b4dbac71. The builder lands; the planner schedules the cohort and deploys.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6803e28cc9c12e972e890855953c703fa61ce61a","binding":"assigned","head":"d73ca9c5bcbcd8a971ef14affc59bbcefad97a9b","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:eafb7667c091e58f0b8df730f43fe31bc2f8e54e\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7e959e256a3bd40094ea85f425f92d179a0bdd12\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:264e71368ec16bd16cfae651c12e4f7cad72e18b","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}